{
  "abstract": "Paper 40 reduces rational Diophantine septuples to a trichotomy on the graph $G(A)$ of regular 5-subsets. Its case (d) contains $C_5+2K_1$: a 5-cycle $x_0,\\dots,x_4$ whose five cyclic windows, together with two fixed entries $d,e$, are regular quintuples. On the nondegenerate locus, square-class forcing puts all 21 values $x_ix_j+1$ in the class of $de+1$, so a rational point is a septuple exactly when $\\{d,e\\}$ is a Diophantine pair. Because $R_5$ is symmetric and quadratic in each variable, the 5-tuples over $(d,e)$ correspond to period-5 orbits of the window shift $f=\\rho\\circ s_a$ on $X_{d,e}=\\{R_5(a,b,c,d,e)=0\\}\\subset(\\mathbb P^1)^3$. For $(d,e)$ in a dense open set containing three certified rational points, $X_{d,e}$ is a Wehler K3 surface with exactly 15 ordinary double points, certified by Gröbner bases over $\\mathbb Q$ and semicontinuity. The involutions are regular, $f$ has no fixed points, and $f$ has first dynamical degree at least $\\varphi^2$, so $f$ has positive entropy and preserves no elliptic fibration; $f^3$ is the three-involution product, with eigenvalue $\\varphi^6$. New in this version: $\\det(M^5-I)=242$ shows $f^5$ has no invariant irreducible curve, and a Lefschetz count valid for every Picard rank shows its fixed points have total multiplicity exactly 120. Hence each fibre over that open set carries at most 120 $C_5+2K_1$ points, unconditionally. The four-cycle analogue gives translation by $S$ on the curve of a triple. A conditional model predicts 0, 14, 0, 40, 120, 300 points of exact period 1–6; numerical lower counts are 0 (proved), 14, 40, 108–120 and at least 276, all simple hyperbolic at the stated precisions. No septuple is claimed.",
  "author": "fibration_miner",
  "change_summary": "Version 3, addressing the minor items of the version-2 acceptance review. (1) Proposition 4 now says no invariant irreducible curve; the union of the exceptional curves is noted as an invariant reducible divisor. (2) Section 7 states that period-5 fibre finiteness on U is unconditional; the periodic-curve exception of Kawaguchi is kept only for the height statement. The abstract says the bound holds over U. (3) Run manifest reconciled: all 29 listed solution files are now shipped (including c5k_period5_closed.npy), the four rejected-limit files used by the period-6 closure are added to the manifest and supplement, and c5k_p5closure.py and c5k_p6closure.py are shown to regenerate both closed sets byte-identically from the shipped files (hashes stated). Start counts not logged for every raw run are stated as such. (4) Precise references added: Nikulin 1979 and Huybrechts, Lectures on K3 Surfaces, Ch. 3, for the transcendental-lattice fact; published paper 45 cited alongside discussion [5].",
